PATCH 144 -- make the list-dialog scroll-arrow auto-repeat dispatch work: retype a85b to a WORD and
recover the DOWN auto-scroll handler FUN_0000_d103.

ROOT CAUSE (runtime-traced).  The cc33 modal loop dispatches its per-frame handler slot a85b
(DGROUP:0xe85b) every frame via `ax=[0xe85b]; call ax` (asm cc9f).  a85b is a WORD near code-offset
(asm cc33 `mov WORD PTR [0xe85b],0xd0d7`; cd8f `mov WORD PTR [0xe85b],0xd103` on a down-arrow press).
Ghidra typed DAT_2000_a85b as undefined4 (DWORD) -- which OVERLAPS the adjacent a85d/a85e -- so the
dispatch read `a85b_word | (a85d,a85e)<<16` (e.g. 0x00ffd103) and fist_icall_far() mapped that bogus
seg:off to garbage -> the trap trampoline (no-op).  d0d7 (idle, empty) happened to look identical to
the no-op, so the working flows were unaffected, but the down-arrow's 0xd103 handler ALSO trapped ->
the scroll never dispatched.  Fix: retype a85b to undefined2 (word); the (uint32_t)a85b dispatch then
zero-extends to linear 0xd0d7/0xd103 and resolves via the fmap.  Init a85b to the near offset 0xd0d7
(matches the asm) instead of a host pointer.

RECOVER FUN_0000_d103 (asm 0xd103..0xd134).  Ghidra recovered FUN_0000_d107 (the throttle tail, taking
the elapsed-tick delta as param_1) and FUN_0000_d0d8 (UP auto-repeat) but not the d103 ENTRY:
    d103: mov ax,[0x452] ; sub ax,[0xe885]      ; ax = ticks since last scroll step
    d10a: cmp ax,8 ; jbe 0xd134                  ; throttle: step only every >8 ticks
    d10f..d134  == FUN_0000_d107(param_1) body   ; update [0xe885]; step [0xe83a] down 1 clamped
So d103 = `FUN_0000_d107((uint16_t)([0x452]-[0xe885]))` -- faithful, no new logic.  Register 0xd103 in
the fmap.  No existing flow holds the down-arrow, so this only adds behaviour on a held down-scroll;
retyping a85b to word leaves the idle dispatch (d0d7, empty) byte-identical (zero regression to the 11
verified flows).

